e check on makespan \u2212 (w\u00b7m + C_ref) is achievable, verifying the reformulated \u00a73.3.\n4. NUMERICAL CONSEQUENCE for the \u03b2-form under our own scenario grid (arithmetic, p=8): derived c_ref vs \u03b2-form: S0 (\u03b21=\u03b22=1) 4.6667 vs 4.6667 (+0.0%); S1 (\u03b2=1.5) 4.9000 vs 4.2000 (\u221214.3%); S2 (\u03b2=3) 4.3750 vs 5.2500 (+20.0%); S7 (\u03b2=3, \u03c9=0.075) 4.2945 vs 5.2500 (+22.2%); \u03c9=0.05 4.5902 vs 4.6667 (+1.7%). Interleaved: v=2 \u2192 2.3333, v=4 \u2192 1.1667, ratio c(1F1B)/c(1F1B-I) = 2.000 / 4.000 exactly \u21d2 P2's \u2248v ratio is preserved by the new reference, no contradiction (researcher's numbers 4.67 and 1.17 reproduced).\n5. INTERLEAVED 1F1B-I CONSTRUCTION NOT YET VALID (why P2 gets NOT EVALUABLE instead of NOT CONFIRMED): two prototype constructions were tried. (a) p\u00b7v virtual stages with invocation durations f/v, \u03b2/v: c grew with m (p=4,v=2: 4.0, 7.0, 13.0, 25.0 for m=8\u202664; p=8,v=4: 28.7\u2192330.8) \u2014 not O(1), so the schedule, not the reference, is wrong (the chain is being double-counted). (b) flattened v\u00b7m virtual microbatches: prototype raised IndexError (device-index bookkeeping) and was not pursued, since fixing it belongs to implementation, not design. Hence the 1/v scaling is an unverified design expectation and is gated by a golden-case check.\n6. NEW DEFECT FOUND BY THE SAME AUDIT (\u03b4>0 breaks the c-reference): with per-stage skew multipliers, the additive C is no longer constant in m \u2014 \u03b4=0.1 seed=1: C(128)=32.38, C(256)=53.41; \u03b4=0.1 seed=2: 36.23\u219265.05; \u03b4=0.2 seed=1: 52.87\u219298.83; \u03b4=0.2 seed=2: 64.46\u2192123.09; while \u03b4=0 stays exactly C=14.000 at both m. Therefore comparing c_fit to c_ref at \u03b4>0 would produce spurious P1/P2 failures; the patch restricts the c_ref comparison to S0/S1/S2 (\u03b4=0) and keeps skew scenarios for monotonicity/differences/paired comparisons only.\n7. P0 RE-BASING ARITHMETIC (same substitution, TP=m/(m+c